---
import ToolLayout from "../../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro";
import { OhmsLawCalculator } from "../../../components/electrical/OhmsLawCalculator.js";
import { getToolByPath } from "../../../content/tools.js";

const tool = getToolByPath("/tools/electrical/ohms-law")!;
---

<ToolLayout
  tool={tool}
  purpose="Calculate amps from volts and ohms. The calculator opens on 12 V and 6 Ω, with current left blank, and the result is 2 A. You can also solve for volts or ohms."
>
  <OhmsLawCalculator client:load />

  <Fragment slot="details">
    <h2>How to calculate current</h2>
    <p>
      Electrical current in amps is voltage divided by resistance: <code>I = V ÷ R</code>. A 12 volt supply across a
      6 ohm resistor draws 12 ÷ 6 = 2 amps. Leave the current box empty and enter the other two values.
    </p>

    <h2>Formula</h2>
    <p><code>V = I × R</code> (voltage = current × resistance), rearranged to solve for whichever value is missing.</p>

    <h2>Accepted inputs</h2>
    <p>
      Exactly two of voltage in volts, current in amps, and resistance in ohms. The blank field is the one that gets solved. The calculator opens with 12 V and 6 Ω, and current blank, so it solves for current.
    </p>

    <h2>How to read the result</h2>
    <p>
      Current is shown in amps, voltage in volts, and resistance in ohms. Power for the same numbers is shown in watts. The label names the value that was blank.
    </p>

    <h2>Worked example</h2>
    <p>
      The opening values, 12 V and 6 Ω, give I = 12 ÷ 6 = 2 A and power = 12 × 2 = 24 W. To solve for resistance instead, clear resistance, enter 12 V and 2 A, and the result is 6 Ω with the same 24 W.
    </p>

    <h2>DC and resistive circuits only</h2>
    <p>
      Ohm's law in this simple form applies to DC circuits and purely resistive AC loads. It does <strong>not</strong>
      apply directly to AC circuits containing inductance or capacitance, where impedance (Z) — a complex quantity
      with magnitude and phase — relates voltage and current instead of plain resistance. Use the
      <a href="/tools/electrical/power/">watts calculator</a> for AC power.
    </p>

    <h2>Assumptions and limitations</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Resistance of 0Ω is treated as invalid when solving for current (it would imply infinite current for any nonzero voltage).</li>
      <li>Current of 0A is treated as invalid when solving for resistance (division by zero).</li>
      <li>Negative resistance values are rejected as physically invalid for this calculator.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Reference</h2>
    <p>
      Ohm's law and the volt, ampere, ohm, and watt are defined within the International System of Units (SI), as
      maintained by NIST and the Bureau International des Poids et Mesures (BIPM).
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
